  "content": {
    "id": "v2-c92fc-p53-practice-slot-4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Find the values of $a$ and $b$ if $(x^2-x-6)$ is a factor of $P(x)=x^3+ax^2+bx+12$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=-3,\\ b=-4$. Factorise $x^2-x-6=(x-3)(x+2)$, then use $P(3)=P(-2)=0$.",
      "worked": "$x^2-x-6=(x-3)(x+2)$, so by the factor theorem $P(3)=0$ and $P(-2)=0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(3)&=27+9a+3b+12=0,\\\\3a+b&=-13,\\\\P(-2)&=-8+4a-2b+12=0,\\\\2a-b&=-2.\\end{align*}$$\nAdding the two equations:\n$$\\begin{align*}5a&=-15,\\\\a&=-3,\\\\b&=-13-3(-3)=-4.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": null
  },
